4. AI Model
Selected Model: SegFormer-B0
Hugging Face Repository:
https://huggingface.co/BiliSakura/xBD-post-bda-models-transformers
Model Checkpoint:
segformer-b0/xbd-baseline/best
This is a pretrained semantic segmentation model fine-tuned on xBD post-disaster satellite imagery.
We selected it because:
It is already trained on building damage data.
It is relatively lightweight.
It does not require training from scratch.
It can run with PyTorch and Hugging Face Transformers.
It produces damage segmentation predictions.
It can run on CPU, although GPU inference is preferable.
Model Input
A post-disaster RGB satellite image.
The original training and evaluation use 1024 × 1024 image tiles.
For inference, apply the normalization specified in the model documentation.
Model Output
The model produces a segmentation mask containing five classes:
Class ID Class
0 Background
1 No Damage
2 Minor Damage
3 Major Damage
4 Destroyed
Every pixel is assigned a predicted class.
The output can be displayed as a colored overlay on top of the satellite image.
Example Model Loading
Use the official checkpoint directly:
import torch
from transformers import SegformerForSemanticSegmentation

MODEL_REPO = "BiliSakura/xBD-post-bda-models-transformers"
MODEL_CHECKPOINT = "segformer-b0/xbd-baseline/best"

device = "cuda" if torch.cuda.is_available() else "cpu"

model = SegformerForSemanticSegmentation.from_pretrained(
    MODEL_REPO,
    subfolder=MODEL_CHECKPOINT
)

model = model.to(device)
model.eval()
Follow the official model documentation for image preprocessing.
The inference pipeline should:
Load the post-disaster image.
Convert it to RGB.
Convert the image to a PyTorch tensor.
Normalize using ImageNet mean and standard deviation.
Run inference using the pretrained model.
Resize the output logits to match the image dimensions.
Apply argmax to obtain the predicted segmentation classes.
Generate a colored damage mask.
The model should be loaded only once and cached.
Do not retrain or fine-tune the model for the first MVP.
Damage Visualization
Use these colors:
Background: Transparent
No Damage: Green
Minor Damage: Yellow
Major Damage: Orange
Destroyed: Red
Display the segmentation result directly over the satellite image.
Provide a slider to control overlay transparency.
Important: This is semantic segmentation. The output represents predicted damage pixels, not automatically verified individual building counts.
Do not display estimated building counts unless an actual building-instance extraction method has been implemented and tested.
5. Zone Priority Ranking
This is the main decision-support feature of our project.
After the AI model generates the damage segmentation mask, the system should automatically divide the image into a 3 × 3 grid.
This creates nine zones:
+----------+----------+----------+
|  Zone A  |  Zone B  |  Zone C  |
+----------+----------+----------+
|  Zone D  |  Zone E  |  Zone F  |
+----------+----------+----------+
|  Zone G  |  Zone H  |  Zone I  |
+----------+----------+----------+
Each zone should receive a damage severity score calculated from the AI predictions.
Ranking Method
Start with a simple weighted scoring algorithm.
Assign damage weights:
Damage Class Weight
No Damage 0
Minor Damage 1
Major Damage 3
Destroyed 5
Calculate the score for each zone using predicted building-damage pixels.
Priority Score =

(1 × Minor Damage Pixels
+ 3 × Major Damage Pixels
+ 5 × Destroyed Pixels)

÷ Total Predicted Building Pixels
Where:
Total Predicted Building Pixels
includes pixels classified as No Damage, Minor Damage, Major Damage, or Destroyed.
Background pixels are excluded.
The score ranges from 0 to 5.
If a zone has no detected building pixels, classify it as "Insufficient Data" rather than Low Priority.
Priority Levels
For the initial prototype, use configurable thresholds:
Score Priority
2.5–5.0 High
1.0–<2.5 Medium
0–<1.0 Low
These thresholds are preliminary design choices, not scientifically validated emergency response standards.

