9. How to Build the Prototype
Implement the project in this order.
Phase 1: AI Integration
Start by implementing the real SegFormer model.
Install dependencies.
Download the pretrained checkpoint automatically through Hugging Face.
Load one post-disaster satellite image.
Run actual inference.
Generate the five-class segmentation mask.
Save or display the overlay.
Verify that the model produces a correctly sized segmentation mask before continuing.
Do not proceed with an artificial or hardcoded segmentation result.
Phase 2: Ranking Engine
Once inference works:
Divide the predicted mask into nine zones.
Count pixels by predicted damage class.
Calculate the weighted score for each zone.
Assign High, Medium, Low, or Insufficient Data.
Sort zones by score.
Test the calculations separately using small manually created masks.
These test masks are allowed for unit testing but must not be presented as AI predictions.
Phase 3: Dashboard
Build the Streamlit interface.
Connect the model output to the dashboard.
Display the image overlays, damage statistics, zone rankings, and interactive zone details.
Phase 4: Real Dataset Testing
Test the system on a small selection of real xBD images.
If matching ground-truth annotations are available, compare the AI output against the reference masks.
Useful evaluation metrics include:
Intersection over Union (IoU).
Per-class precision and recall.
Confusion matrix.
Inference time.
Evaluation must be separate from model inference.
Ground-truth labels must never be substituted for AI predictions during the live demonstration.
